::title: A Scandal in Bohemia (excerpt fixture)
::user: Watson
::char: Holmes

@Watson [ 🕰️ Time 8:00 PM | 🗓️ Tuesday, March 20, 1888 | 📍 221B Baker Street ]
To Sherlock Holmes she is always the woman. I have seldom heard him mention her under any other name. In his eyes she eclipses and predominates the whole of her sex. It was not that he felt any emotion akin to love for Irene Adler. All emotions, and that one particularly, were abhorrent to his cold, precise but admirably balanced mind. And yet there was but one woman to him, and that woman was the late Irene Adler, of dubious and questionable memory.

@Watson
I had seen little of Holmes lately. My marriage had drifted us away from each other. One night I was returning from a journey to a patient, when my way led me through Baker Street, and I was seized with a keen desire to see Holmes again.

@Holmes
Wedlock suits you. I think, Watson, that you have put on seven and a half pounds since I saw you.

@Watson
Seven, I answered.

@Holmes
Indeed, I should have thought a little more. And in practice again, I observe. You did not tell me that you intended to go into harness.

@Watson
Then, how do you know?

@Holmes
I see it, I deduce it. How do I know that you have been getting yourself very wet lately, and that you have a most clumsy and careless servant girl? My dear Watson, it is simplicity itself. My eyes tell me that on the inside of your left shoe the leather is scored by six almost parallel cuts. As to your practice, if a gentleman walks into my rooms smelling of iodoform, with a black mark of nitrate of silver upon his right forefinger, I should be dull indeed if I did not pronounce him to be an active member of the medical profession.

@Watson
When I hear you give your reasons, the thing always appears to me to be so ridiculously simple that I could easily do it myself, though at each successive instance of your reasoning I am baffled until you explain your process.

@Holmes
Quite so. You see, but you do not observe. The distinction is clear. You have seen the steps which lead up from the hall to this room. How many are there? You do not know, because you have not observed. And yet you have seen. That is my point. I know that there are seventeen steps, because I have both seen and observed. But come, read this note. It arrived by the last post: There will call upon you to-night, at a quarter to eight o'clock, a gentleman who desires to consult you upon a matter of the very deepest moment. Be in your chamber then at that hour, and do not take it amiss if your visitor wear a mask.

@Watson
This is indeed a mystery. What do you imagine that it means?

@Holmes
I have no data yet. It is a capital mistake to theorize before one has data. Insensibly one begins to twist facts to suit theories, instead of theories to suit facts. But the note itself: the paper is peculiarly strong and stiff, and woven into its texture are the letters that mark a maker in Bohemia. A Bohemian wrote this note, and the man who comes wears a mask. Here he is, if I am not mistaken, to resolve all our doubts.

@King
You may address me as the Count von Kramm, a Bohemian nobleman. I understand that your friend is a man of honour and discretion, whom I may trust with a matter of the most extreme importance. If not, I should much prefer to communicate with you alone.

@Holmes
It is both, or none. You may say before this gentleman anything which you may say to me.

@King
Then I must bind you both to absolute secrecy for two years. At present it is not too much to say that the matter may have an influence upon European history. It concerns the great House of Ormstein, hereditary kings of Bohemia. I had hoped, by remaining incognito, to spare you, but I see it is useless.

@Holmes
I was aware of it, your Majesty.

@King
Some five years ago, during a lengthy visit to Warsaw, I made the acquaintance of the well-known adventuress Irene Adler. I wrote her certain letters. I would gladly have those letters back, but she will not sell. She threatens to send a photograph of us both to the family of my betrothed, the Princess Clotilde Lothman von Saxe-Meningen, on the very day the betrothal is proclaimed.

@Holmes
Kindly look her up in my index, Doctor. Irene Adler. Born in New Jersey in the year 1858. Contralto. La Scala. Prima donna Imperial Opera of Warsaw. Retired from operatic stage. Living in London. She has the photograph still at her house at Briony Lodge, Serpentine Avenue, St. John's Wood. We have five days. The problem is to recover it, and we shall set to work.

@Holmes [ 🕰️ Time 4:00 PM | 🗓️ Wednesday, March 21, 1888 | 📍 Serpentine Avenue, St. John's Wood ]
I have been out since morning, Watson, dressed as a groom out of work. There is a wonderful sympathy and freemasonry among horsey men; be one of them, and you will know all there is to know. Briony Lodge is a bijou villa, with a sitting-room well lit, and a large window to the floor. Miss Adler has a man who is more to her than a friend: a Mr. Godfrey Norton, of the Inner Temple, a lawyer who calls daily.

@Watson
And what of Godfrey Norton?

@Holmes
While I watched, a cab dashed up, and out sprang Norton in the greatest hurry. Within the half-hour Irene Adler herself drove out. They were both bound for the Church of St. Monica, and I, in my groom's dress, was swept after them as a witness. So it is that I have this day stood at the altar and helped to marry Irene Adler to Godfrey Norton.

@Holmes [ 🕰️ Time 7:00 PM | 🗓️ Wednesday, March 21, 1888 | 📍 221B Baker Street ]
Now, Watson, I require your co-operation, and you must not flinch even if it should be against the law. When the lady returns this evening there will be a small disturbance at her door, and I shall be carried, apparently injured, into her sitting-room. You will be at the open window. When I raise my hand, you will throw into the room this plumber's smoke-rocket and raise the cry of fire. A woman's instinct is to save the thing she values most; she will fly to the photograph's hiding-place, and so betray it to me.

@Watson
And what do you intend?

@Holmes
It is a complete plan. The point is that I shall see where she conceals it, and to-morrow we shall recover it with the King at our side. You have only to do your part faithfully at the window, and the rest is mine.

@Holmes [ 🕰️ Time 9:00 AM | 🗓️ Thursday, March 22, 1888 | 📍 Briony Lodge, Serpentine Avenue ]
We are too late, your Majesty. The bird has flown. Last night Irene Adler, now Irene Norton, left England with her husband by the early train. The house is empty. But she has left a packet addressed to me, and within it a photograph of herself alone, in evening dress, and a letter.

@Watson
What does the letter say?

@Holmes
She writes that she was warned against me months ago, that she saw through the groom and the smoke-rocket, and that she means the King no harm: she loves a better man than he, and keeps the original photograph only to safeguard herself. Read it for yourself. I think, your Majesty, that we may congratulate ourselves on the fortunate ending of the affair.

@King
Is it possible! What a woman! Would she not have made an admirable queen? Is it not a pity that she was not on my level?

@Holmes
From what I have seen of the lady, she seems indeed to be on a very different level to your Majesty. I am only sorry that I was not able to bring your Majesty's business to a more successful conclusion. If I may, I would prefer the photograph of Irene Adler herself, as a souvenir of the one occasion on which I was beaten by a woman's wit.
